PSU Patching Orchestration Workflow
Automation Owners: Huiyuan Li (PSU precheck and apply script), George Barclay (OEM Integration)
Phases of the workflow
•	Precheck – Take list of services running on server / perform tar backup of Oracle homes / Run analyze operation on Grid/Database Home
•	Apply – Perform validation of precheck status / place DB monitoring in blackout / Capture list of invalid objects prior to patch / Apply PSU Patch on Grid and Database home per iteration / Compare status of services and object status / Remove services from blackout
Manual steps to follow
•	Software for OPatch / PSU binaries need to be downloaded and placed in centralized server – xusvga92409d002
•	Create a placeholder file on centralized server which has name of script files / software files
•	Server list targeted for patching (Sat/Sun) need to be created and placed in each OMS server to allocate them under OEM groups.
High-level workflow of this automation
Precheck phase (before downtime window)
•	Segregate list of servers to be patched on Saturday/Sunday and add it to relevant OEM groups in different regions (US/EMEA/Japan).
•	Validate SSH connectivity between OMS and target DB server to ensure central server can connect.
•	Transfer software to target server where we have more space by creating a work directory
•	Perform cleanup on target server work directory to ensure it doesn’t have old files.
•	Take necessary pre-checks/tar level backups on homes prior to starting PSU precheck operations.
•	Perform PSU patch analyze on Oracle Grid/Database software homes by transferring the software/opatch files from centralized location
•	Perform PSU patch analyze for OJVM patch on Oracle Grid/Database software homes.
•	Based on the result of analyzing operation, update the status on flag file and send mail to Oracle team DL.
Apply phase (during downtime window)
•	Ensure the precheck flag file is successful, else stop the operation and report to end user.
•	If precheck is successful, place DB services in blackout at OEM and comment the crontab scripts
•	Perform patching on Grid home and ensure all services are up post patching.
•	Proceed to apply DB Release update and OJVM update on RDBMS home and run sqlpatch to update registry details of database. Compare the invalid object list and ensure it matches. If any failures or mismatch observed post DB patching, report the same to DBA team and mark the step as failure.
•	Enable OEM monitoring and uncomment the crontab scripts.
What is not possible in this automation? 
•	Windows server hosting Oracle databases
•	Servers that are having conflicts with PSU software.
Precheck phase
Step 1 – Software downloads to centralized server (one time)
•	Download PSU and latest OPatch software from https://support.oracle.com by referring to document ID - Oracle Patch master note 
•	Use WinZip or any related tool to copy from local desktop to server – xusvga92409d002 to path - /backup/ORACLE/PATCH
 
Step 2 – Create or update placeholder file on centralized server (one time)
•	Go to location - /export/PSU_DETAILS on server xusvga92409d002
•	Update properties PSU_PATCH_FILE, OJVM_PATCH_FILE, OPATCH_FILE, TAR_BACKUP_FILE, SCRIPT_FILE_GRID, SCRIPT_FILE_DB, OPATCH_VERSION as per latest file names to use for patch cycle.
 
•	PSU_PATCH_FILE – Name of the release update file downloaded from Oracle 
•	OJVM_PATCH_FILE – Name of the OJVM update file downloaded from Oracle
•	OPATCH_FILE – Name of the Opatch folder update file downloaded from Oracle
•	TAR_BACKUP_FILE – Name of the script file to copy to target to take tar backups
•	SCRIPT_FILE_GRID – Name of the script file to copy to target server to apply Grid patch
•	SCRIPT_FILE_DB– Name of the script file to copy to target server to apply RDBMS patch
•	OPATCH_VERSION – Version value which Oracle recommends as per Readme file of latest patch

